<!-- SECTION 1: Introduction -->
<section id="missing-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find a Missing Angle in a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>If one acute angle of a right triangle measures <span set:html={rich('[[35^\\circ]]')} />, the missing angle is <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ-35^\\circ=55^\\circ]]')} />. That single subtraction is the whole calculation. A right triangle always contains one 90-degree angle, so the two remaining angles must share exactly the 90 degrees that are left over. Subtracting the known angle from 90 degrees reveals the missing one, with no side lengths and no trigonometric functions required.</p>
  <p>The answer, 55 degrees in this case, represents the other acute angle in the same right triangle. The two angles always work as a pair: knowing either one determines the other completely. This calculator exists for exactly that situation, when an angle is already known and only its complement is missing. When no angle is known yet, an <a href="/right-triangle-angle-from-tangent-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">angle from tangent calculator</a> or one of its sine and cosine counterparts derives the first angle from measured sides.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: Understanding angles -->
<section id="missing-angles" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Are the Angles in a Right Triangle?</h2>

  <h3>The Right Angle</h3>
  <p>A 90-degree angle represents a perfect corner, the angle between a vertical wall and a level floor. In every right triangle this angle is fixed: it never changes size, and diagrams mark it with a small square at the vertex. Because one angle is permanently set to 90 degrees, the other two angles have only 90 degrees left to share between them, which forces both to be acute.</p>

  <h3>The Two Acute Angles</h3>
  <p>An acute angle measures more than <span set:html={rich('[[0^\\circ]]')} /> and less than <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />. Both remaining angles of a right triangle fall in this range, and they are called complementary because their measures always add to 90 degrees. The pairing is strict: a single known acute angle leaves exactly one possible value for its partner, so the missing angle is never a matter of estimation.</p>

  <h3>What Are Complementary Angles?</h3>
  <p>Two angles are complementary when their measures add up to <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />. The pairs <span set:html={rich('[[30^\\circ+60^\\circ=90^\\circ]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[35^\\circ+55^\\circ=90^\\circ]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ+45^\\circ=90^\\circ]]')} /> are all complementary. Complementary angles do not need to be equal; equality happens only at 45 degrees each. Any two positive angles that total 90 degrees qualify, whether they sit inside the same triangle or not.</p>

  <h3>Why the Angles of a Triangle Add Up to 180 Degrees</h3>
  <p>Every triangle, whatever its shape, contains exactly 180 degrees across its three interior angles. In a right triangle the fixed right angle claims 90 of those degrees immediately, which leaves precisely 90 degrees for the two acute angles combined. The diagram below shows the full accounting for a triangle with angles of 35, 55, and 90 degrees.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('missing-angles.svg')} alt="Right triangle labeled with angles A 35 deg, B 55 deg, C 90 deg" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Angles A (35 deg) and B (55 deg) are complementary: A + B = 90 deg, and 35 + 55 + 90 = 180 deg.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: Formula derivation -->
<section id="missing-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>The Formula for Finding a Missing Angle</h2>
  <p>The formula comes straight from the triangle angle-sum rule. Start with the general statement:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A+B+C=180^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Because the triangle is right-angled, the third angle is fixed:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[C=90^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Substitute that value into the sum:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A+B+90^\\circ=180^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Subtract 90 degrees from both sides, and the two acute angles stand alone:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A+B=90^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Finally, subtract the known angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> from both sides to isolate the missing angle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[B=90^\\circ-A]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Each step is ordinary algebra applied to a geometric fact. Nothing is memorized blindly: the 90 degrees in the formula is the share of the angle sum left over after the right angle takes its half.</p>
  <p>The same relationship works in reverse. If angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> is the known one, solving for <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> gives:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A=90^\\circ-B]]]')} /></p>
  <p>This calculator uses the first form because its input field accepts angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and returns angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. The two formulas are not different rules; they are the same complementary-angle relationship solved for different unknowns.</p>

  <h3>Why 90 Degrees, Not 180 Degrees</h3>
  <p>A frequent error is subtracting the known angle from 180 degrees. That total covers all three angles of the triangle, so using it alone forgets that the right angle has already been accounted for. With <span set:html={rich('[[A=35^\\circ]]')} />, the wrong computation gives <span set:html={rich('[[180^\\circ-35^\\circ=145^\\circ]]')} />, which cannot be an acute angle of any right triangle. The correct computation, <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ-35^\\circ=55^\\circ]]')} />, respects the right angle and can be checked at once: <span set:html={rich('[[35^\\circ+55^\\circ+90^\\circ=180^\\circ]]')} />.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('missing-complementary.svg')} alt="Two panels contrasting the 180 deg triangle sum with the 90 deg acute pair" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Part A shows the full triangle sum; Part B isolates the complementary acute angles.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: Calculator walkthrough -->
<section id="missing-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Missing Angle Calculator</h2>
  <p>The walkthrough below matches the actual interface on this page.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the right triangle.</strong> Confirm the shape has a genuine 90-degree corner before doing anything else; the formula only applies to right triangles.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the acute angle you already know.</strong> This is angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, the value you will type into the calculator.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm the known angle is valid.</strong> It must be greater than <span set:html={rich('[[0^\\circ]]')} /> and less than <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />. Anything outside that range cannot be an acute angle of a right triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the known angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> in degrees.</strong> The input field accepts decimal values such as 68.5, so keep the precision of your measurement.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate Angle B.</strong> The tool applies <span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-A]]')} /> to your input.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the calculated complementary angle.</strong> The output is angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> in degrees.</li>
    <li><strong>Add the two acute angles to check the result.</strong> The sum must equal 90 degrees; if it does not, recheck the input.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset when you want to start a new calculation.</strong> This clears the input so a fresh angle can be entered.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Notice what the calculator never asks for: no opposite-side length, no adjacent-side length, no hypotenuse, and no sine, cosine, or tangent values. One known acute angle is the entire input because the angle-sum rule does the rest. The tool also does not accept radians or a direct entry of angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />; its input field is specifically angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> in degrees.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5: Four foundational examples -->
<section id="missing-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Finding Missing Angles</h2>
  <p>These four examples build the core skill before the real-world applications. Each follows the same pattern: write the formula, substitute, subtract.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: A Known Angle of 35 Degrees</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[A=35^\\circ]]')} />, find angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Step 1: Write the formula. <span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-A]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 2: Substitute the known angle. <span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-35^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 3: Subtract. <span set:html={rich('[[B=55^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>Check: <span set:html={rich('[[35^\\circ+55^\\circ=90^\\circ]]')} />. The answer is valid because the two acute angles total exactly 90 degrees and each sits strictly between 0 and 90 degrees.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: A Known Angle of 20 Degrees</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[A=20^\\circ]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-20^\\circ=70^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>A smaller acute angle produces a larger complementary angle. Because the pair must always total 90 degrees, every degree removed from one angle is added to the other. Here the 20-degree angle leaves a generous 70 degrees for its partner.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: An Isosceles Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[A=45^\\circ]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-45^\\circ=45^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>Both acute angles are equal at 45 degrees each. This is the isosceles right triangle, the only right triangle whose acute angles match. Its two legs are also equal in length, which is why the 45-45-90 triangle appears so often in drafting and construction layouts.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: A Known Angle of 68.5 Degrees</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[A=68.5^\\circ]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-68.5^\\circ=21.5^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>A relatively large acute angle has a relatively small complement. The decimal input works exactly like a whole-number input: subtract carefully, keeping the .5 aligned, and 90 minus 68.5 gives 21.5. The check still holds: <span set:html={rich('[[68.5^\\circ+21.5^\\circ=90^\\circ]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6: Twelve practical examples -->
<section id="missing-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Applications of Missing Angles</h2>
  <p>One point needs care before the examples begin. The missing-angle formula applies directly when two angles belong to the same right triangle. In practical settings, a horizontal surface and a vertical surface often serve as the perpendicular reference lines, and a diagonal component such as a ladder or rafter forms the hypotenuse. The examples below state that assumption wherever it is used.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Finding a Roof Rafter Angle to the Vertical</h3>
  <p>A roof rafter makes an angle of <span set:html={rich('[[35^\\circ]]')} /> with the horizontal. Find the angle between the same rafter and a vertical reference line.</p>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[A=35^\\circ]]')} /> measured from the horizontal.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-35^\\circ=55^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The horizontal and vertical reference lines are perpendicular, so the two angles are complementary. The 55-degree result is the angle relative to the vertical, not a complete roof-pitch or structural calculation.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Finding a Ladder Angle to a Wall</h3>
  <p>A ladder rests against a vertical wall and forms an angle of <span set:html={rich('[[68^\\circ]]')} /> with the level ground. Find the angle between the ladder and the wall.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-68^\\circ=22^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The ground and the wall form a right angle in this idealized model, with the ladder as the hypotenuse. The 22-degree angle describes the ladder orientation relative to the wall. The calculation alone does not establish whether the ladder is safely positioned.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Understanding a Ramp Angle to the Vertical</h3>
  <p>A straight ramp makes an angle of <span set:html={rich('[[8^\\circ]]')} /> with the horizontal. Find the complementary angle between the ramp direction and a vertical reference line.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-8^\\circ=82^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The ramp angle to the vertical is the complement of its angle to the horizontal. A shallow 8-degree incline leaves a steep 82-degree angle against the vertical. This angle alone does not determine accessibility or code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: Finding a Staircase Stringer Complementary Angle</h3>
  <p>A staircase stringer makes an angle of <span set:html={rich('[[32^\\circ]]')} /> with the floor. Calculate its angle relative to a vertical reference line.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-32^\\circ=58^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The floor, the vertical reference, and the diagonal stringer form the right triangle. The 58-degree result describes the stringer orientation against the vertical. It does not verify staircase dimensions or building-code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: Calculating a Diagonal Brace Angle to a Vertical Support</h3>
  <p>A diagonal brace makes an angle of <span set:html={rich('[[40^\\circ]]')} /> with a horizontal beam. Find the angle between the brace and a perpendicular vertical support.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-40^\\circ=50^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The two reference directions must be perpendicular for the complement rule to apply. The 50-degree angle is a pure geometry result; it does not determine the structural strength of the brace.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Finding a Guy Wire Angle to a Vertical Pole</h3>
  <p>A guy wire makes an angle of <span set:html={rich('[[30^\\circ]]')} /> with level ground. Find the angle between the wire and a vertical pole.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-30^\\circ=60^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The horizontal ground and the vertical pole define the right angle, with the wire as the hypotenuse. The 60-degree result reframes the same wire direction against the vertical reference. It does not establish the wire tension or anchor safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: Converting an Elevation Angle to Its Complement</h3>
  <p>A surveying diagram records a line of sight at an elevation angle of <span set:html={rich('[[24^\\circ]]')} /> above the horizontal. Calculate its complementary angle relative to the vertical.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-24^\\circ=66^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>An angle measured from the horizontal and an angle measured from the vertical describe the same line of sight from perpendicular viewpoints. The example assumes a straight line of sight with perpendicular horizontal and vertical references.</p>

  <h3>Example 12: Finding a Roof Slope Angle to a Vertical Reference</h3>
  <p>A roof slope is measured at <span set:html={rich('[[27^\\circ]]')} /> relative to the horizontal. Find its complementary angle to the vertical.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-27^\\circ=63^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The two measurements describe the same slope from different perpendicular reference directions. Neither measurement alone is a structural assessment.</p>

  <h3>Example 13: A Diagonal in a Square Drafting Layout</h3>
  <p>A diagonal line in a square drafting layout makes an angle of <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /> with one side. Find the angle it makes with the adjacent perpendicular side.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-45^\\circ=45^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The diagonal bisects the square right angle, so both resulting angles are 45 degrees. This relationship helps with basic drawing and layout checks: if a measured diagonal does not split the corner evenly, the layout is not square.</p>

  <h3>Example 14: Finding a Machine Panel Complementary Angle</h3>
  <p>A panel is tilted <span set:html={rich('[[15^\\circ]]')} /> away from the horizontal. Calculate the angle between the panel orientation and the vertical reference direction.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-15^\\circ=75^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The horizontal and vertical references are perpendicular, so the panel tilt and its complement describe the same orientation. The result describes orientation only, not machine safety or performance.</p>

  <h3>Example 15: Converting a Tree Observation Angle</h3>
  <p>A person looks toward the top of a tree at an angle of elevation of <span set:html={rich('[[38^\\circ]]')} /> above the horizontal. Find the complementary angle between the line of sight and a vertical reference direction.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-38^\\circ=52^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The calculation changes the reference direction, not the line of sight itself. The 52-degree angle is simply the same gaze measured against the vertical. It does not calculate the tree height.</p>

  <h3>Example 16: Finding a Handrail Angle to a Vertical Support</h3>
  <p>A diagonal handrail makes an angle of <span set:html={rich('[[55^\\circ]]')} /> with a horizontal landing. Find its complementary angle relative to a vertical support line.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-55^\\circ=35^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The complementary angle describes the same diagonal orientation from a different reference. A steep 55-degree angle against the horizontal becomes a shallow 35-degree angle against the vertical. The angle alone does not verify accessibility or construction requirements.</p>

