<!-- SECTION 7: Relationship between complementary angles -->
<section id="missing-relationship" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Relationship Between Complementary Angles</h2>
  <p>The pattern is perfectly predictable. If angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> increases, angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> decreases by exactly the same amount, because their total is locked at 90 degrees. If <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> decreases, <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> grows to fill the gap. At <span set:html={rich('[[A=45^\\circ]]')} /> the two meet in the middle, and neither acute angle can reach <span set:html={rich('[[0^\\circ]]')} /> or <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} /> in a genuine right triangle with three distinct vertices.</p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Known angle A</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Formula</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">Missing angle B</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">10 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">90 - 10</td><td class="py-2.5">80 deg</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">20 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">90 - 20</td><td class="py-2.5">70 deg</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">30 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">90 - 30</td><td class="py-2.5">60 deg</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">45 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">90 - 45</td><td class="py-2.5">45 deg</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">60 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">90 - 60</td><td class="py-2.5">30 deg</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">75 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">90 - 75</td><td class="py-2.5">15 deg</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">80 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">90 - 80</td><td class="py-2.5">10 deg</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The table is symmetrical around 45 degrees: the pairs (10, 80), (20, 70), and (30, 60) mirror each other because swapping the known and missing angles just reads the same complementary relationship from the other side. The four panels below make this trade-off visual.</p>

