<!-- SECTION 8: Reverse formula -->
<section id="missing-reverse" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What If You Know Angle B Instead?</h2>
  <p>The same complementary-angle rule works in reverse. If angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> is the known one, solve the relationship for <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A=90^\\circ-B]]]')} /></p>
  <p>No new formula is required; it is the identical equation with the roles exchanged.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[B=62^\\circ]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A=90^\\circ-62^\\circ=28^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>Three more short reverse-direction examples follow the same step:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><span set:html={rich('[[B=15^\\circ]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[A=90^\\circ-15^\\circ=75^\\circ]]')} />.</li>
    <li><span set:html={rich('[[B=42^\\circ]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[A=90^\\circ-42^\\circ=48^\\circ]]')} />.</li>
    <li><span set:html={rich('[[B=70^\\circ]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[A=90^\\circ-70^\\circ=20^\\circ]]')} />.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One practical note: this calculator input field accepts angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and returns angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. A reader who knows only angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> should apply the reverse formula by hand unless the interface is verified to support entering <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> directly.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 9: Complementary vs supplementary -->
<section id="missing-vs" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Complementary Angles vs. Supplementary Angles</h2>
  <p>The two terms sound alike and both describe angle pairs, but they answer different questions.</p>

  <h3>Complementary Angles</h3>
  <p>Two angles are complementary when their sum is <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />. The two acute angles of a right triangle are the textbook example: they must be complementary because the right angle already claims the other 90 degrees of the triangle total.</p>

  <h3>Supplementary Angles</h3>
  <p>Two angles are supplementary when their sum is <span set:html={rich('[[180^\\circ]]')} />. A straight line, for instance, can be split into two supplementary angles. Supplementary pairs should never be confused with the two acute angles of a right triangle, which sum to 90, not 180.</p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Relationship</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Total</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Complementary</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">90 deg</td><td class="py-2.5">35 + 55</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Supplementary</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">180 deg</td><td class="py-2.5">110 + 70</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Right angle</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">90 deg</td><td class="py-2.5">The fixed angle in a right triangle</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The full triangle angle sum is 180 degrees, but the two acute angles together sum to 90 degrees. Keeping those two totals separate is the key to avoiding the most common mistake on this page.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('missing-applications.svg')} alt="Four panels showing complementary angles for a ladder, rafter, brace, and square diagonal" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Complementary pairs in real settings: each known angle determines its partner.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 10: Common mistakes -->
<section id="missing-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Finding a Missing Angle</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Subtracting the known angle from 180 degrees instead of 90.</strong> The 180-degree total includes the right angle, which is already fixed. What goes wrong: with <span set:html={rich('[[A=35^\\circ]]')} /> the wrong math gives 145 degrees, an impossible acute angle. Fix: subtract from 90, giving <span set:html={rich('[[55^\\circ]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting that one angle is already 90 degrees.</strong> The right angle is easy to overlook because it needs no calculation. What goes wrong: treating the triangle as if all three angles were unknown. Fix: remember the right angle claims half the 180-degree total before you begin.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering 90 degrees into the calculator.</strong> The tool expects an acute angle strictly between 0 and 90 degrees. What goes wrong: a 90-degree input leaves no room for a second acute angle. Fix: enter only the known acute angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering an angle greater than 90 degrees.</strong> No acute angle exceeds 90 degrees by definition. What goes wrong: the subtraction yields a negative result, which has no geometric meaning here. Fix: check that the input is below 90 degrees.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering 0 degrees.</strong> A zero angle collapses the triangle into a line segment. What goes wrong: the result claims a 90-degree complement for a shape with no area. Fix: use only positive acute angles.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing complementary angles with supplementary angles.</strong> Complementary pairs sum to 90 degrees; supplementary pairs sum to 180. What goes wrong: subtracting from 180 produces the mistake in item 1. Fix: match the word to the total, complementary with 90.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming the two acute angles must be equal.</strong> Equality holds only at 45 degrees each. What goes wrong: expecting symmetry in a 20-70 triangle. Fix: let the subtraction decide; most pairs differ.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing up angle labels A and B.</strong> The calculator takes <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and returns <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. What goes wrong: entering the already-known complement as if it were the input. Fix: label the known angle A before typing anything.</li>
    <li><strong>Thinking that side lengths are required for this particular calculation.</strong> Sides matter for trigonometry, not for complements. What goes wrong: stalling on a problem that needs only subtraction. Fix: if an acute angle is known, subtract it from 90 and finish.</li>
    <li><strong>Using inverse sine, cosine, or tangent when simple subtraction is sufficient.</strong> Inverse functions answer a harder question. What goes wrong: extra work and extra chances for mode errors. Fix: when an angle is known, the <a href="/right-triangle-angle-from-sine-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">angle from sine calculator</a> and its siblings are the wrong tools; subtraction is exact and immediate.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming a measured physical angle automatically forms a right triangle without checking the reference directions.</strong> Real corners are rarely perfect. What goes wrong: applying the complement rule where the 90-degree corner does not actually exist. Fix: verify the reference directions are perpendicular before subtracting.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding an input measurement unnecessarily before subtracting.</strong> Early rounding throws away precision the input actually had. What goes wrong: 68.5 rounded to 69 gives 21 instead of 21.5. Fix: subtract with the full measured value, then round the result to match the input precision.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[A=35^\\circ]]')} />:</p>
  <p>Incorrect: <span set:html={rich('[[180^\\circ-35^\\circ=145^\\circ]]')} />. This is not the missing acute angle because the right angle has not been accounted for; 145 degrees cannot sit inside a right triangle alongside a 90-degree corner.</p>
  <p>Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ-35^\\circ=55^\\circ]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Verify: <span set:html={rich('[[35^\\circ+55^\\circ+90^\\circ=180^\\circ]]')} />. The full angle sum confirms the answer.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 11: Verification -->
<section id="missing-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Missing Angle Is Valid</h2>
  <p>The valid input range is:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[0^\\circ \\lt A \\lt 90^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>and the calculated angle must satisfy the same range:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[0^\\circ \\lt B \\lt 90^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>An input of 0 or 90 degrees would produce a degenerate limiting case rather than an ordinary right triangle with three distinct vertices and positive side lengths. The calculator enforces the strict inequality for exactly this reason.</p>

  <h3>Method 1: Add the Acute Angles</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[A+B=90^\\circ]]')} />. Example: <span set:html={rich('[[35^\\circ+55^\\circ=90^\\circ]]')} />. This is the fastest check and catches the 180-degree mistake immediately.</p>

  <h3>Method 2: Check the Complete Triangle</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[A+B+90^\\circ=180^\\circ]]')} />. Example: <span set:html={rich('[[35^\\circ+55^\\circ+90^\\circ=180^\\circ]]')} />. This confirms the answer against the universal triangle angle sum.</p>

  <h3>Method 3: Check the Reverse Calculation</h3>
  <p>If <span set:html={rich('[[B=90^\\circ-A]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[A=90^\\circ-B]]')} /> must recover the original input. With <span set:html={rich('[[A=35^\\circ]]')} /> giving <span set:html={rich('[[B=55^\\circ]]')} />, the reverse check is <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ-55^\\circ=35^\\circ]]')} />. This consistency check needs no side measurements at all.</p>
  <p>One caution: if the input angle comes from a physical measurement, its uncertainty carries into the result. A roughly measured 35 degrees yields a roughly known 55 degrees; report the answer with precision that matches the measurement. For checks involving side lengths, the <a href="/pythagorean-theorem-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Pythagorean theorem calculator</a> can confirm the triangle geometry independently.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 12: Choosing a calculator -->
<section id="missing-choose" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Choosing the Right Right Triangle Calculator</h2>
  <p>Each tool below answers a different question. Match the tool to what you actually know:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Right Triangle Missing Angle Calculator</strong> (this page): finds the complementary angle when one acute angle is already known. Use it whenever the input is an angle, not a side.</li>
    <li><a href="/right-triangle-angle-from-sine-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline"><strong>Right Triangle Angle From Sine Calculator</strong></a>: finds an acute angle from the opposite side and the hypotenuse. Use it when those two lengths were measured.</li>
    <li><a href="/right-triangle-angle-from-cosine-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline"><strong>Right Triangle Angle From Cosine Calculator</strong></a>: finds an acute angle from the adjacent side and the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><a href="/right-triangle-angle-from-tangent-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline"><strong>Right Triangle Angle From Tangent Calculator</strong></a>: finds an acute angle from the opposite and adjacent sides, the two legs.</li>
    <li><a href="/right-triangle-side-from-sine-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline"><strong>Right Triangle Side From Sine Calculator</strong></a>: finds a missing side length when an angle and one side are known.</li>
    <li><a href="/right-triangle-side-from-cosine-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline"><strong>Right Triangle Side From Cosine Calculator</strong></a>: finds a missing side length from an angle and the hypotenuse or adjacent side.</li>
    <li><a href="/right-triangle-side-from-tangent-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline"><strong>Right Triangle Side From Tangent Calculator</strong></a>: finds a missing leg from a known angle and the other leg.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The decision rule is simple. You know one acute angle: use the missing-angle calculator on this page. You know two suitable side lengths: use the matching inverse-trigonometric calculator. You need an unknown side: use a side calculator. And you need the second angle after a trigonometric calculation: subtract the known angle from 90 degrees, which is exactly what this page automates.</p>
